tag when needed"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying punctuation independently.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about dialogue with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\n\"Can I help?\" asked the shopkeeper.\n\n\"Yes, please,\" said Mina.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nWrite two lines where a child asks a price and a shopkeeper answers.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nEach speaker begins on a new line with their own quotation marks.","explain":"A new paragraph for a new speaker helps readers follow the conversation.\n\nA new paragraph for a new speaker helps readers follow the conversation. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nRunning two speakers into one paragraph makes turn-taking unclear. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Start a new line when the speaker changes"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 5, Lesson 3: Punctuating dialogue","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Narrative: story elements and planning","week":5,"week_in_u